<%# Follow / block control for `user` (the profile owner being viewed). Self-wraps
    in dom_id(user, :follow_button) so the follow/block Turbo Streams replace it in
    place. Only shown to a signed-in non-owner. button_to lives on the main routes
    (CSRF on) so the generated form carries the authenticity token.
    States (Slice 7): blocked-by-me → Unblock (never Follow); blocked-by-them →
    Block only (never Follow for a hidden pair); otherwise Follow/Following + Block.

    DESIGN SYSTEM (Slice 9, Task 4.4) —
    docs/design-system/components/social/FollowButton.prompt.md: "the follow/block pair
    on a profile header. Designed for the indigo background only." That last clause is
    why every button below takes `on_primary` / `on_primary_outline` rather than the
    house `outline` pill: those two variants are locked to white-on-primary and to the
    small size precisely because there is exactly one surface they may appear on, and
    this partial is it. They take no `tone:` — asking for one is a category error.

    Filled (`on_primary`) means "the relationship exists": Following, Requested,
    Unblock. Outlined (`on_primary_outline`) means "act": Follow, Block. The four
    branches below are the prompt's own rules, unchanged.

    The secondary Block that rides alongside Follow/Following carries `opacity-80` to
    keep it quieter than the control it sits next to — it used to say that with a
    `border-white/<pct>` plus a narrower `px-<n>`, which is why it looks different now.
    Opacity is the one utility that can express the same idea without fighting the
    variant: a border-opacity or a padding override would be the SAME Tailwind family
    as the variant's own border and padding, and same-family utilities resolve by
    bundle order rather than call order (see `ui/_card`'s `padded:` header for the full
    trap). Nothing in `ds_button_classes` sets a base opacity, so `opacity-80` has
    nothing to collide with. Note the placeholder spelling of the two dead classes
    above: Tailwind scans these files as plain text and would keep emitting rules for
    utilities no view renders any more.

    FROZEN: every `button_to` path and HTTP verb. `unfollow_user_path` with DELETE is
    reused by BOTH "Following" (drop an accepted follow) and "Requested" (cancel a
    pending one) — that is deliberate, not a copy-paste. `dom_id(user, :follow_button)`
    is the replace target for all four of follows/blocks create+destroy turbo streams. %>
<div id="<%= dom_id(user, :follow_button) %>">
  <% if user_signed_in? && current_user.id != user.id %>
    <% if current_user.blocks_made.exists?(blocked_id: user.id) %>
      <%= button_to unblock_user_path(user.username), method: :delete,
            class: ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary) do %>
        <%= lucide_icon("user-x", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
        <span>Unblock</span>
      <% end %>
    <% elsif current_user.hidden_user_ids.include?(user.id) %>
      <%# They blocked me — never offer Follow. Block-back stays available. %>
      <%= button_to block_user_path(user.username),
            class: ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary_outline) do %>
        <%= lucide_icon("ban", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
        <span>Block</span>
      <% end %>
    <% else %>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
        <% if current_user.following.include?(user) %>
          <%= button_to unfollow_user_path(user.username), method: :delete,
                class: ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary) do %>
            <%= lucide_icon("user-check", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
            <span>Following</span>
          <% end %>
        <% elsif current_user.active_follows.pending.exists?(followed_id: user.id) %>
          <%# Private target with a pending request (Slice 7b). The button cancels the
              request — reuses the unfollow route (DELETE removes the pending follow). %>
          <%= button_to unfollow_user_path(user.username), method: :delete,
                class: ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary) do %>
            <%= lucide_icon("clock", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
            <span>Requested</span>
          <% end %>
        <% else %>
          <%= button_to follow_user_path(user.username),
                class: ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary_outline) do %>
            <%= lucide_icon("user-plus", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
            <span>Follow</span>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
        <%= button_to block_user_path(user.username),
              class: "#{ds_button_classes(variant: :on_primary_outline)} opacity-80" do %>
          <%= lucide_icon("ban", class: "w-4 h-4") %>
          <span>Block</span>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</div>
